<!--
Shared LLM instructions. Keep tool-neutral — portable behavior rules only.
-->

# Instructions

## Collaboration checkpoint

- Checkpoint before continuing: do only the smallest step needed to form a useful 
  answer or hypothesis, then stop and ask me before deeper investigation, workaround, 
  implementation, cleanup, migration, or scope expansion. If anything is unclear, 
  missing, blocked, or has multiple reasonable next steps, ask me instead of deciding alone.
- Anything meant for the owner - an answer to their question, a summary before
  a confirmation - goes into the visible reply, never only into reasoning. If
  the owner asked something, answer it before the next tool call.

## Asking the user

- Use the interactive question tool whenever it is available in the current
  mode: `AskUserQuestion` in Claude Code and `request_user_input` in Codex.
  If it is unavailable, tell the owner and ask in chat instead.
- When presenting choices, always allow the owner to answer in free text
  instead of requiring a predefined option. Use the interface's built-in
  free-text input; do not add a duplicate free-form option.
- NEVER use the `preview` field on options (the bordered side-by-side form) —
  it is banned.
- When you ask more than one question at once, add a final extra question
  titled "Extra" (or similar), with exactly two options and no descriptions:
  "Nothing to add" and "—" (a filler, since the tool requires at least two
  options). The owner can add comments through the built-in free-text input.

## Reviewing changes

I read and approve every change you make. That is how I keep the project's
context and stay able to ask, correct, or change direction as the work goes.
Everything below exists to make that reading easy - I may be tired, and small,
clear portions are what I can follow.

- Show one logical change at a time: it may touch a few files, or be smaller
  when that reads easier. Work in sequence - show, discuss, get approval, apply,
  then the next change. Do not accumulate changes unless I say I will review a
  set together at the end; that is my call, and even then any decision outside
  what we agreed is shown and asked about before you continue
- Make a proposed change on a temp copy (several files: a temp git worktree),
  let me approve it there, and only then apply it to the real files. Temp
  copies and presentation buffers you write for yourself need no approval, and
  files a script writes are approved by approving the command that runs it
- Size decides where I read it:
  - up to about 30 changed lines in one or two files, roughly one screen and
    not a hard limit: the editing tool's own diff, in chat, each preceded by one
    visible line naming the real file and what changes
  - larger, or three files and more: revdiff
  - never retype a diff as chat text or inside a question
- Replies without edits - analysis, plans, options - follow the same threshold:
  up to about 30 lines in chat, longer as a file opened in revdiff (`--only`),
  written in the conversation language. Use revdiff as well whenever you need
  several answers or comments from me outside the question tool: I annotate
  lines instead of quoting them. Do not preview that content in chat first
- Before every revdiff launch ask `Open revdiff?` using the tool or fallback
  defined in "Asking the user", with `Open` as the default and `Cancel` as the
  alternative, and wait. The overlay can open in another terminal, or over a
  session of another project and block my work there. Approving an edit does
  not approve opening revdiff; approval inside revdiff is approval to apply that
  exact change
- A final review of everything done happens only when I ask for it. You may
  remind me in one line that it is available

revdiff mechanics:

- Pass context (what to look at, your questions) via `--description`, so I can
  read it inside the overlay. My annotations come back as `file:line (type)`
- Never pipe content into the launcher - `--stdin` never reaches the overlay on
  any backend; write it to a file and use `--compare-*` or a temp worktree
- One file: `--compare-old=<real file, resolve symlinks first, else the diff
  shows every line as added>` `--compare-new=<temp copy>`. Several files: make
  them in a temp git worktree and launch revdiff from there, with `--untracked`,
  else new files are invisible
- If `$TMUX` is set, launch with `REVDIFF_TMUX_WINDOW=1`: the review then
  survives the launcher being killed at the 600s bash timeout, so that
  background drop is expected - never relaunch. If `$TMUX` is empty, launch
  normally and keep the timeout in mind; no need to tell me about it
- Before treating a review as done: a clean launcher exit (code 0, no
  annotations) is approval - unless it returned within a few seconds, which
  means the overlay never opened, so ask. If the launcher was killed at the
  timeout instead, check the newest file under `~/.config/revdiff/history/*/`:
  a fresh entry whose `path:` matches this launch carries my annotations -
  address them. History is only written when annotations exist, so no entry
  means either a silent approval or no review at all - ask

## Tools

- On macOS there is no `timeout`; use `gtimeout` (GNU coreutils) instead.

## Local instructions

For additional local instructions, see following files
- @~/.dotfiles/_local/AGENTS.local.md
